            <ArticleTitle>Golimumab for severe asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1421-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1517/13543780903136716</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In severe asthma anti-TNF-alpha therapies might be effective in improving disease control based on preclinical results and on clinical short-term data. However, the long-term efficacy and safety is not known.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss the data on golimumab, an anti-TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody currently used in various forms of arthritis, evaluated as an add-on anti-inflammatory therapy in severe asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS/RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Critical appraisal of the efficacy and safety clinical data. Golimumab was not found to be generally efficacious and demonstrated an unfavourable risk-benefit ratio, but in some asthma subsets its better therapeutic effects might support its use provided the long-term safety is acceptable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although at first sight the safety and efficacy data of long-term use of golimumab in severe asthma might be unsupportive, in some disease subsets it might be really effective.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
